# Authenticated appliance TLS

New controller records and connection probes verify certificates by default.
The TrueNAS and hypervisor onboarding dialogs also start with verification on.
Existing records are not silently rewritten: review any `verify_ssl=false`
controllers before upgrading and deploy their trusted certificate chain.

TrueNAS REST and WebSocket clients and UniFi Protect default to verified TLS.
TrueNAS uses HTTPS even on nonstandard ports; legacy port 80 maps to 443, just
as its WebSocket and transfer channels do. A WebSocket certificate failure is
terminal, not a reason to try REST. Authenticated HTTP redirects are refused.
TrueNAS downloads accept only an appliance-relative `/_download/` URL, never
a response-selected host or HTTP URL carrying the API key.

## Private appliance certificates

Use certificates whose subject alternative names match the configured host.
For a private CA, provide a PEM CA certificate bundle (no private keys), set
`FREESDN_DEVICE_CA_BUNDLE=/run/freesdn-device-ca/ca.pem`, and bind-mount that file
read-only into every backend service that connects to the appliance. This adds
trust to the system store; an unreadable or invalid configured bundle fails
closed. Recreate affected services to apply changed environment or mounts.

Example Compose override for the API; repeat the volume on `worker`, optional
`worker-io`, and `api-2` when those services perform these operations. Include
any other service that actually connects to the appliance in your deployment.

```yaml
services:
  api:
    volumes:
      - type: bind
        source: ./secrets/device-ca.pem
        target: /run/freesdn-device-ca/ca.pem
        read_only: true
        bind:
          create_host_path: false
```

The base Compose environment forwards `FREESDN_DEVICE_CA_BUNDLE` to backend
services. The certificate file must be readable by the non-root application
user. This setting currently applies to TrueNAS REST/WebSocket/transfers and
UniFi Protect; it is not a claim that every other integration uses this policy.
These HTTP clients ignore ambient proxy environment variables. TrueNAS REST
retains the configured overlay-aware client factory; live VPN acceptance is
still required for every advertised transport/topology.

## Existing insecure configurations

TrueNAS's explicit `verify_ssl=false` option remains for reviewed legacy
installations and emits a warning. Encryption without peer verification does
not authenticate the device and should not be the production default. Enabling
verification may expose a missing CA, wrong hostname or expired certificate;
correct those conditions rather than enabling an automatic insecure retry.
The camera service's UniFi Protect path always verifies TLS and uses the CA
bundle above; it no longer silently opts out. Other camera transports retain
their separate protocol/security limitations and still need review.

The loopback tests in `tests/adapters/test_appliance_tls.py` cover trusted,
unknown-CA, expired and wrong-host certificates; credentials do not reach a
rejected peer. Redirect refusal and transfer-channel trust are also exercised.
These are transport tests with synthetic credentials, not new vendor-hardware
qualification. Certificate and endpoint changes need a staged acceptance run
against the actual supported appliances before production rollout.
